course: dictionary
lesson: 8877
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Shoulder is a good example: it can mean a part of your body, the edge of a road, or a verb meaning to carry something or take on a job. The words around it tell you which meaning to choose. In «high shoulders behind it», the shoulders are clearly on a body, because they sit behind a head.
explain: Read the whole sentence before you decide. «His shoulders humped, his eyes fixed on the straight grey road.» Shoulders that can hump belong to a body, and the road is what his eyes are on. Try your meaning in the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have chosen well.
explain: Shoulder can also be a verb, so it changes its form: shoulders, shouldered, shouldering. When you see «shouldered», look for a thing being carried or a job or blame being taken on. Those clues will show you the verb meaning.
fact: Shoulder is a noun (a shoulder, shoulders) and a verb (to shoulder, shouldered, shouldering).
fact: The shoulder is the part of your body where your arm joins the rest of your body, below your neck.
fact: The shoulder of a road is the strip at the edge where cars can stop. This use is mostly found in US, Canadian and Australian English.
fact: To shoulder something means to carry it on your shoulder, or to take on a job or blame.
fact: «Shoulder to shoulder» means side by side. Your shoulder is a ball-and-socket joint, so your arm can move in a circle.
exercise: In «Badger peered out of a hedge, with high shoulders behind it», what are the shoulders? | Two bags of seeds | Heavy loads he is carrying | The edges of a road | *Parts of Badger's body where his arms join
exercise: «I will shoulder the blame for the broken vase.» What does shoulder mean here? | Part of an arm | The edge of a road | To tap someone on the back | *To take on the blame
exercise: «The collarbone often breaks from a fall on the shoulder.» Where is the shoulder? | *Where the arm joins the body, below the neck | A strip at the edge of a road | A bag strap | The top of the head
test: «Dad shouldered the heavy box up the stairs.» What does shouldered mean? | Shrugged at it | Opened it | *Carried it on his shoulder | Dropped it on the floor
test: «The friends walked to school, shoulder to shoulder.» What does this mean? | Arguing about the road | *Side by side | Carrying their bags | Far apart
exam: «The lorry pulled onto the shoulder while the driver checked the tyre.» What does shoulder mean here? | To carry something | To shrug | *The edge of a road where a vehicle can stop | Part of a body
